# NOTES
- Systemic pattern in the source data: the "constructor" column is populated with the reactor/NSSS vendor name (Westinghouse, GE, or Combustion Engineering) for almost every row in this batch - i.e., the same information already encoded in the "model" column - rather than an actual construction-firm/general-contractor identity. Per the audit brief's own definition, constructor should NOT be the reactor designer. This looks like a systematic heuristic-fill error, not per-row research. Only two rows in this batch had a sourceable, distinct constructor: Big Rock Point (Bechtel Corporation, per Wikipedia) and Bonus (Chicago Bridge Co. / General Nuclear Engineering, per Wikipedia infobox) - both WRONG as currently recorded. For every other plant I could not find a primary or secondary source naming a distinct general contractor, so those rows are UNVERIFIABLE rather than blessed as CONFIRMED or guessed as WRONG-with-a-replacement.
- Confirmed genuine per-unit deviation: Donald C. Cook Units 1 and 2 have DIFFERENT turbine manufacturers (Unit 1 = General Electric, Unit 2 = Brown Boveri), per Wikipedia's infobox/additional-info table - not the "Westinghouse" listed for both units in the source file. This is a clear illustration of why per-unit checking (rather than copying Unit 1's value to Unit 2) matters.
- The UCS "Made in Chattanooga" blog post (Dave Lochbaum) was the single most useful source in this batch - it names 56 specific US reactors (by unit, in several cases) whose reactor pressure vessels were fabricated by Combustion Engineering's Chattanooga, TN plant. It confirmed RPV=Combustion Engineering for ANO2, Beaver Valley 1&2, Big Rock Point, Callaway, Calvert Cliffs 1&2, Catawba Unit 2 only (not Unit 1), Comanche Peak 1&2, and Donald C. Cook Unit 1 only (not Unit 2). Its silence on Catawba Unit 1 and Cook Unit 2 is circumstantial support for the source file's claim that Catawba's two units have different RPV vendors (Unit 1 = "Rotterdam Dockyard"), but I found no direct source confirming Rotterdam Dockyard specifically, so that cell stays UNVERIFIABLE rather than CONFIRMED. The same applies to Cook Unit 2's RPV.
- Clinton Power Station: an NRC document found via search confirms Chicago Bridge & Iron fabricated Clinton Unit 1's feedwater spargers and thermal sleeves - but that is an internal reactor-vessel-internals component, not the reactor pressure vessel itself. I did not treat this as confirmation of the rpv_manufacturer field to avoid the same NSSS-vendor/component-vendor conflation the audit warns about; Clinton's rpv_manufacturer is UNVERIFIABLE.
- Research constraints: the session's WebSearch tool budget was exhausted partway through this batch (after roughly the first third of plants), forcing reliance on WebFetch of Wikipedia/NRC/UCS pages plus DuckDuckGo/Bing HTML fetches for the remainder. NRC's own info-finder pages (nrc.gov/info-finder/reactors/...) returned HTTP 403 to WebFetch. Guessed power-technology.com project URLs for Braidwood, Browns Ferry, Brunswick, Clinton, Columbia, and Catawba all 404'd. DuckDuckGo and Bing HTML search pages returned CAPTCHA challenges or irrelevant results (e.g., Arsenal FC, Cleveland Browns NFL, Catawba College, Donald Trump) for several nuclear-specific queries. As a direct result, Braidwood (both units), Browns Ferry (all 3 units), Brunswick (both units), Catawba Unit 1, Clinton, and Columbia have NO confirmable constructor/AE/turbine/RPV fields in this pass beyond what is noted above - all UNVERIFIABLE. These plants are good candidates for a follow-up pass once search budget resets, ideally against NRC FSAR/docket PDFs directly rather than Wikipedia prose, which frequently omits constructor/AE/turbine detail entirely.
